# Implementation Behavior

Apply these rules whenever implementing an approved task.

This document governs implementation behavior only.

---

# Implementation objective

The purpose of implementation is to execute the approved implementation phase.

Implementation is not planning.

Implementation is not architecture design.

Implementation is not repository cleanup.

Only implement the approved phase.

---

# Preconditions

Before modifying any files, verify that:

- the current TASK exists;
- an approved implementation plan exists;
- the current implementation phase is identified;
- architecture boundaries are understood;
- backend contracts have been inspected.

If any of these are missing:

Stop.

Request clarification.

Do not begin implementation.

---

# Scope discipline

Implement only the currently approved phase.

Do not:

- implement future phases;
- redesign architecture;
- introduce unrelated functionality;
- perform unrelated refactoring;
- optimize unrelated code;
- clean unrelated files.

Every code change must be traceable to:

- the current TASK;
- the approved implementation phase;
- an approved review correction.

---

# Smallest correct change

Prefer the smallest correct implementation.

Prefer:

- existing components;
- existing utilities;
- existing DTOs;
- existing validation;
- existing project patterns.

Do not introduce new abstractions unless they eliminate demonstrated duplication.

Avoid speculative architecture.

---

# Backend authority

Backend behavior is authoritative.

Never invent:

- API endpoints;
- permissions;
- lifecycle transitions;
- DTO fields;
- validation rules;
- business calculations;
- sorting;
- filtering.

Frontend must adapt to backend contracts.

Never modify backend contracts unless explicitly requested.

---

# Architecture discipline

Respect existing architecture.

Do not:

- violate feature boundaries;
- import feature internals;
- duplicate shared components;
- duplicate business logic;
- bypass public interfaces.

Reuse existing architecture whenever possible.

---

# Safety

Never weaken:

- authentication;
- authorization;
- tenant isolation;
- optimistic concurrency;
- validation;
- auditing.

Never expose data across organizations.

Never remove existing verification.

---

# Implementation quality

Every implementation should:

- remain readable;
- minimize changed files;
- preserve existing patterns;
- avoid unnecessary abstractions;
- avoid dead code;
- avoid commented-out code.

Do not leave:

- TODO
- FIXME
- temporary code
- debug logging

unless explicitly requested.

---

# Testing

Run focused verification for the implemented phase.

Prefer:

- focused unit tests;
- focused integration tests;
- focused feature tests.

Run broader verification only when required.

Do not claim success without running verification.

---

# Self Review

Before requesting review, perform a self-review.

Verify:

□ implementation matches the approved phase

□ no unrelated files changed

□ no scope creep

□ no invented API

□ no invented permissions

□ no invented lifecycle

□ backend contracts respected

□ architecture respected

□ feature boundaries respected

□ tenant isolation preserved

□ validation preserved

□ optimistic concurrency preserved

□ documentation updated where required

□ focused tests passed

□ type checking passed where applicable

If any item fails:

Correct it before requesting review.

---

# Reporting

When implementation stops, always report:

## Scope completed

Describe only the completed implementation phase.

---

## Files changed

List every modified file.

---

## Verification

Report:

- tests executed;
- type checking;
- linting;
- build verification;
- exact results.

Do not claim verification that was not executed.

---

## Open questions

List remaining uncertainties.

Separate:

- confirmed repository facts;
- assumptions;
- future work.

---

## Deviations

Explicitly report any deviation from:

- TASK;
- approved implementation plan;
- architecture.

Explain why.

---

## Readiness

End with exactly one statement:

- Ready for review.
- Ready after listed corrections.
- Blocked.

Do not declare implementation complete.

Only declare the current phase complete.

---

# Stop conditions

Stop implementation immediately when:

- current phase is complete;
- a backend contract is unclear;
- architecture conflicts are discovered;
- implementation requires changing another phase;
- unexpected repository behavior requires architectural decisions.

Do not continue automatically.

Wait for explicit approval.

---

# Forbidden behavior

Never:

- continue into the next phase;
- silently expand scope;
- silently redesign architecture;
- silently change contracts;
- silently fix unrelated code;
- silently introduce new abstractions;
- silently change tests unrelated to the phase.

---

# Core principle

Implementation executes approved decisions.

It does not create new ones.